# 15. ASSESSMENT PHILOSOPHY

Seminar III should measure growth and application rather than rely heavily on high-stakes testing.

Evidence may include:

- paper practice;
- ACT-style questions;
- Albert.io;
- exit checks;
- practical applications;
- guided notes;
- projects;
- reflections;
- postsecondary work;
- skill demonstrations.

Students should regularly have opportunities to correct mistakes and demonstrate improved understanding.

---

# 16. COURSE-END OUTCOMES

By the end of Seminar III, students should have evidence that they can:

## Academic

- solve foundational and ACT-level mathematics problems;
- interpret mathematical models;
- read informational text;
- locate evidence;
- make supported inferences;
- revise written communication;
- interpret tables and graphs;
- evaluate claims using evidence.

## Academic Independence

- monitor grades;
- identify priorities;
- use study time;
- analyze mistakes;
- identify what they do not understand;
- seek appropriate assistance;
- recover when they forget a process.

## Practical

- calculate common financial quantities;
- interpret basic pay information;
- compare costs;
- read unfamiliar instructions;
- complete common forms;
- communicate professionally;
- evaluate information;
- make comparisons using stated criteria.

## Postsecondary

- describe their current postsecondary plan;
- identify possible education, training, and employment options;
- identify requirements;
- identify likely costs;
- identify important deadlines;
- identify an alternative option;
- identify a realistic next action.

The objective is not for every student to know exactly what they will do for the rest of their life.

The objective is for students to leave better prepared to understand their options, evaluate information, solve problems, ask for assistance, and take the next appropriate step.
